(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-hearing-taste-smell-quiz"]={"title":"The Sense of Hearing, Taste, and Smell — Physiology Exam 2","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the tympanic membrane amplify sound before it reaches the cochlea?","choices":["It generates resonant vibrations of its own that add extra energy independent of incoming sound waves","Its surface area is about 17 times larger than the oval window, concentrating the same force over a smaller area","It has no amplifying effect at all, since the ossicles alone provide all impedance matching","It amplifies only high-frequency sounds above 4000 Hz, leaving low frequencies unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tympanic membrane transmits vibrations to the cochlea and amplifies the signal because its area is about 17 times larger than the oval window, concentrating force onto the smaller oval window. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the function of the attenuation reflex, involving the stapedius and tensor tympani muscles?","choices":["A loud noise triggers reflex contraction of both muscles, causing ossicular rigidity that attenuates vibration to the cochlea (reducing transmission by 30-40 decibels), protecting the cochlea","A loud noise triggers relaxation of both muscles, increasing ossicular mobility and amplifying sound transmission by roughly 30 to 40 decibels","It only responds to the high-frequency sounds above 4000 Hz, providing essentially no protective attenuation whatsoever for any lower-frequency noise sources","To amplify soft sounds by relaxing ossicular tension, substantially increasing auditory sensitivity during otherwise quiet listening conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"The attenuation reflex is triggered by loud noise, causing reflex contraction of the stapedius and tensor tympani muscles, which makes the ossicular system rigid and attenuates vibration transmission to the cochlea by 30-40 decibels, protecting the cochlea and dampening sounds like one's own voice. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is notable about the size of the stapedius muscle?","choices":["It is among the fastest-contracting skeletal muscles in the body","It is entirely absent in roughly 1% of the general human population","It is the smallest skeletal muscle in the body (about 1 mm long)","It is one of the longest skeletal muscles, at nearly 3 cm in length"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stapedius, which attaches to the stapes and participates in the attenuation reflex, is the smallest skeletal muscle in the human body at about 1 mm long. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What are the three coiled tubes (separated by membranes) that make up the cochlea?","choices":["Organ of Corti, tectorial membrane, and basilar membrane","Scala tympani, scala media, and scala vestibuli","Malleus, incus, and stapes","Cochlear duct, semicircular canal, and utricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cochlea is a system of three coiled tubes separated by membranes: the scala tympani, scala media, and scala vestibuli; sound waves move the tympanic membrane and stapes, displacing cochlear fluid and inducing vibration in the basilar membrane. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What generates the nerve impulses that encode auditory information, and where is this structure located?","choices":["The tectorial membrane alone, generating impulses through direct mechanical contact with the stapes footplate","Hair cells with stereocilia in the organ of Corti, located on the surface of the basilar membrane, shearing against the overlying tectorial membrane","The oval window, located at the base of the cochlea, via direct piezoelectric stimulation of adjacent hair cells","The round window, located near the apex and close to the helicotrema, through the vibration of its own thin membrane surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"The organ of Corti, lying on the surface of the basilar membrane, contains hair cells with stereocilia; movement of the basilar membrane causes the stereocilia to shear back and forth against the overlying tectorial membrane, generating the nerve impulses. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What percentage of auditory signals are transmitted by inner hair cells, despite there being 3-4x as many outer hair cells?","choices":["~50%","~10%","100%, with outer hair cells contributing nothing","~90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"About 90% of auditory signals are transmitted by inner hair cells, even though there are 3-4 times as many outer hair cells; outer hair cells are thought to control the sensitivity of inner hair cells for different sound pitches. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does basilar membrane fiber stiffness change from the base (near the oval window) to the apex (near the helicotrema)?","choices":["Fiber length decreases toward the apex, while stiffness increases roughly 100-fold from base to apex","Stiffness remains constant throughout, with resonant frequency determined solely by fiber length alone","Stiffness decreases about 100-fold toward the apex, with high-frequency resonance near the base and low-frequency resonance near the apex","Stiffness increases about 100-fold toward the apex, with the low-frequency resonance occurring near the base"],"answer":2,"exp":"Basilar membrane fibers increase in length and decrease in diameter from the base (oval window) to the apex (helicotrema), with overall stiffness decreasing about 100-fold — high-frequency resonance occurs near the stiffer base, and low-frequency resonance occurs near the more flexible apex. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the function of the round window in the cochlea?","choices":["It decompresses acoustic energy that enters the cochlea via stapes movement against the oval window","It generates the initial receptor potential via its own dedicated population of hair cells","It is the site where the tectorial membrane attaches to the modiolus of the cochlea","It amplifies incoming sound vibrations before they reach the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The round window serves to decompress (relieve pressure from) the acoustic energy that enters the cochlea via stapes movement against the oval window, allowing fluid within the cochlea to move. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the \"place principle\" explain about hearing?","choices":["How the sensations of taste and smell combine centrally to produce the overall perception of flavour","How sound direction is determined, based on intensity and timing differences between the two ears","How the frequency (pitch) of a sound is perceived, based on which position along the basilar membrane is maximally stimulated","How the loudness of a sound is perceived, based on the total number of hair cells activated"],"answer":2,"exp":"The place principle determines the frequency of sound perceived: different sound frequencies cause the basilar membrane to oscillate maximally at different positions, and the position along the membrane where hair cells are stimulated determines the perceived pitch. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What determines the perceived amplitude (loudness) of a sound?","choices":["The number of ossicles involved in transmission","How much the basilar membrane is displaced","The position along the basilar membrane that is stimulated","The size of the tympanic membrane alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"While the place principle (position along the basilar membrane) determines perceived pitch, sound amplitude (loudness) is determined by how much the basilar membrane is displaced. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How is the decibel unit of sound defined, and why is a logarithmic scale used?","choices":["A decibel represents roughly a 2-fold increase in sound energy; a linear scale is used since the ear's dynamic range is quite limited","Decibels measure the frequency of a sound wave in cycles per second, entirely unrelated to its intensity or energy content","A decibel is a purely linear unit of pressure measured directly in pascals, with no logarithmic scaling involved at all","A 10-fold increase in sound energy equals 1 bel (10 decibels); a logarithmic scale is used because of the extreme range of sound intensities the ear can detect"],"answer":3,"exp":"A 10-fold increase in sound energy equals 1 bel, and 0.1 bel is a decibel (a 1.26-fold increase in energy); sound intensity is expressed logarithmically because of the extreme range of sound intensities encountered, and the ear can barely distinguish a 1-decibel change. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is the auditory cortex organized, and what happens when it is destroyed?","choices":["Arranged by tonotopic maps (high frequency at one end, low at the other); destruction of the auditory cortex causes loss of discrimination of sound patterns","Organized by loudness maps only, not frequency; destruction eliminates the ability to perceive any sound intensity","Organised somatotopically, much like the sensory cortex; destruction of that area abolishes conscious pitch perception entirely","Destruction only affects the ability to hear high-frequency sounds, leaving low-frequency discrimination fully intact"],"answer":0,"exp":"The auditory cortex and association areas are arranged by tonotopic maps, with high-frequency sounds represented at one end and low-frequency at the other; discrimination of sound patterns is lost when the auditory cortex is destroyed. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How do the lateral and medial superior olivary nuclei each determine the direction of a sound?","choices":["Lateral nuclei detect direction via differences in sound intensity between the two ears; medial nuclei detect direction via the time lag between signals reaching each ear","Both the lateral and medial nuclei rely exclusively on time-lag differences, with intensity differences playing no role at all","Both lateral and medial nuclei rely exclusively on intensity differences, with timing cues playing no role in either pathway","Neither the lateral nor the medial nucleus contributes to sound localisation, which instead depends entirely upon the inferior colliculus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior olivary nucleus divides into lateral nuclei (detect direction via differences in sound intensity between the two ears) and medial nuclei (detect direction via the time lag between acoustic signals arriving at each ear). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"By what point in development is the cochlea completely developed and at adult size?","choices":["Only fully developed by puberty, continuing to enlarge gradually throughout childhood","Not until several years after birth, once myelination of the auditory nerve is complete","The cochlea continues to grow slowly throughout adulthood, reaching final size around age 40","By the middle of gestation, with no further gain in size or shape expected after birth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cochlea is completely developed by the middle of gestation and has reached adult size — no further gain in size or change in shape is expected after birth. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the two broad categories of hearing loss mentioned?","choices":["Conductive and neurological","Only neurological hearing loss exists","Only conductive hearing loss exists","Central and peripheral only, unrelated to the cochlea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hearing loss can be categorized as conductive (problems with sound transmission through the outer/middle ear) and/or neurological (problems with the cochlea or auditory nerve/pathway). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Approximately how long does the attenuation reflex take to activate?","choices":["40-80 msec","Instantaneously (0 msec)","Several seconds","Several minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"The attenuation reflex takes about 40-80 msec to activate, meaning it provides some protection against sustained loud sounds but not against very sudden, brief ones. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How can humming reduce the perceived loudness of an unwanted external sound (e.g., someone else talking)?","choices":["Humming physically blocks the eustachian tube, reducing sound conduction by about 15 decibels","Humming activates only the tensor tympani muscle, producing negligible sound attenuation of about 2 decibels","Humming activates the stapedius reflex, which can reduce sound transmission to the cochlea by about 20 decibels","Humming has no effect on sound perception, since the stapedius reflex responds only to sounds above 90 decibels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Humming (producing your own low-frequency voice sound) works through the stapedius reflex, which can produce about a 20-decibel reduction in sound transmission to the cochlea, dampening the perceived loudness of other sounds like someone else's voice. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What three bones make up the ossicular chain connecting the tympanic membrane to the cochlea?","choices":["Malleus, incus, and stapes","Tectorial membrane, basilar membrane, and organ of Corti","Scala tympani, scala media, and scala vestibuli","Cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tympanic membrane is connected to the ossicles — malleus, incus, and stapes — which transmit vibrations from the eardrum to the oval window of the cochlea. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What happens to a hair cell when its stereocilia bend in one direction versus the opposite direction?","choices":["Only bending toward the tallest stereocilium produces any measurable receptor potential change","Bending in one direction causes depolarization; bending in the opposite direction causes hyperpolarization","Bending in either direction produces an identical hyperpolarization, with no depolarizing response","Bending has no electrical effect on hair cells unless it exceeds a fixed mechanical threshold angle"],"answer":1,"exp":"When stereocilia are bent in one direction, hair cells depolarize; when bent in the opposite direction, they hyperpolarize — this bidirectional sensitivity is the beginning of neural transduction of the auditory signal. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Approximately how many stiff, reed-like fibers project from the modiolus to make up the basilar membrane?","choices":["~300,000","~3,000","~3 million","~30,000"],"answer":3,"exp":"The basilar membrane contains approximately 30,000 fibers projecting from the modiolus (the bony center of the cochlea); these stiff, reed-like fibers vibrate at different frequencies depending on their position (place principle). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why is most of what people perceive as \"taste\" actually derived from smell?","choices":["Because the tongue can only differentiate texture and five basic tastes (sweet, sour, bitter, salty, umami), so complex flavor perception mostly comes from olfaction","Because taste buds can detect thousands of distinct flavor compounds directly, without any contribution from olfactory receptors","Because smell and taste rely on entirely identical G-protein-coupled receptor mechanisms, converging upon the very same brainstem nucleus","Taste is entirely independent of smell in humans, relying solely on the five basic taste qualities detected by the tongue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because the tongue can only indicate texture and differentiate between sweet, sour, bitter, salty, and umami, most of what is perceived as the sense of taste is actually derived from smell. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Approximately how many possible chemical receptor types are found in taste buds?","choices":["5","13","50","100"],"answer":1,"exp":"Taste buds contain up to 13 possible chemical receptor types, including two for sodium, two for potassium, chloride, adenosine, inosine, two sweet receptors, two bitter receptors, glutamate, and hydrogen ion. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What causes the perception of sour taste?","choices":["H+ ions from acids (e.g., citric acid, acetic acid)","Long-chain nitrogenous alkaloids like quinine","Glutamate and related amino acid compounds","Ionized sodium and potassium salts mainly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sour taste is caused by H+ ions from acids such as citric acid and acetic acid, with a threshold for citric acid of about 2 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What chemical is primarily responsible for umami taste, and what does \"umami\" mean?","choices":["Sucrose; umami means \"sweet\" in Japanese","Quinine; umami means \"bitter\" in Japanese","Sodium chloride; umami means \"salty\" in Japanese","Glutamate; umami means \"delicious\" in Japanese"],"answer":3,"exp":"Umami taste (Japanese for \"delicious\") is primarily caused by glutamate, with a threshold for MSG (monosodium glutamate) of less than 10 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is bitter taste sensation considered a survival mechanism, and who is exceptionally sensitive to it?","choices":["Bitter taste has no survival relevance at all, existing only as a byproduct of overlapping sour and salty receptor activation","Only elderly individuals show heightened bitter sensitivity, since childhood taste buds have not yet fully matured","Because salty substances are usually toxic in high doses; elderly adults show the greatest sensitivity to them","Because many poisons (alkaloids like quinine and strychnine) taste bitter, and babies/young children are exceptionally sensitive to bitter taste"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bitter taste (caused by long-chain nitrogen-containing organic substances/alkaloids like quinine and strychnine) is a survival mechanism since many poisons taste bitter; babies and young children are exceptionally sensitive to bitter taste, and thresholds for bitter compounds (e.g., strychnine at 0.0001 mM) are far lower than for other taste qualities. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What percentage of taste buds are found on circumvallate papillae, and where are they located?","choices":["75%, concentrated mainly along the tongue's anterior tip","25%, located along the flat dorsal surface of the tongue","50%, forming a V shape on the posterior surface of the tongue","10%, scattered across the soft palate and epiglottis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Circumvallate papillae, which form a V shape on the posterior surface of the tongue, contain about 50% of all taste buds; foliate papillae (lateral tongue surfaces) and fungiform papillae (flat tongue surface) each contain about 25%. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the function of filiform papillae, the most numerous papillae type on the tongue?","choices":["They exclusively detect bitter and sour tastes, containing the highest density of taste buds","They give the tongue its texture and are responsible for the sensation of touch, containing no taste buds","They contain the majority of taste buds and mediate the tongue's fine touch sensation as well","They are found only on the posterior tongue, forming the boundary of the circumvallate papillae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Filiform papillae are the most numerous lingual papillae, fine cone-shaped structures covering most of the tongue's dorsum; they give the tongue its texture and are responsible for touch sensation, rather than taste. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the approximate lifespan of an individual taste bud?","choices":["10 months","10 days","10 hours","10 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"The average life of a taste bud is about 10 days, reflecting rapid turnover of these sensory structures; the human tongue has 3,000-10,000 taste buds total. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the cellular mechanism of bitter taste transduction differ from salty, sweet, sour, and umami transduction?","choices":["Bitter taste causes an internal release of Ca2+, with no external calcium required, unlike the other four taste qualities which depolarize via different mechanisms","Bitter taste does not involve calcium signalling of any kind, relying instead purely upon direct voltage-gated sodium channel activation","Bitter taste requires external calcium entry exactly like sour taste, both relying on the same acid-sensing ion channel","All five taste qualities use an identical G-protein pathway requiring external calcium entry through voltage-gated channels"],"answer":0,"exp":"While salty, sweet, sour, and umami tastes cause depolarization of taste cells via different mechanisms, bitter taste transduction causes an internal release of Ca2+, with no external calcium required — and bitter taste sensation is far more sensitive on a molar basis than other taste sensations. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve transmits taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and which transmits from the posterior one-third?","choices":["Anterior 2/3 via the trigeminal nerve (V); posterior 1/3 via the facial nerve","Anterior 2/3 via the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX); posterior 1/3 via the facial nerve (VII)","Both regions are transmitted by the vagus nerve (X)","Anterior 2/3 via the facial nerve (VII); posterior 1/3 via the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is transmitted via the facial nerve (CN VII), while taste from the posterior one-third is transmitted via the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX); posterior mouth structures are innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X). All signals converge on the solitary nucleus before relaying to the thalamus and cortex. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
